    "reading_order": 10,
    "text": "4.3 Solution of a Quadratic Equation by Factorisation",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b11",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      47.99,
      214.29,
      420.02,
      266.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 11,
    "text": "Consider the quadratic equation 2x2 – 3x + 1 = 0. If we replace x by 1 on the\nLHS of this equation, we get (2 × 12) – (3 × 1) + 1 = 0 = RHS of the equation.\nWe say that 1 is a root of the quadratic equation 2x2 – 3x + 1 = 0. This also means that\n1 is a zero of the quadratic polynomial 2x2 – 3x + 1.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b12",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      269.94,
      420.02,
      336.23
    ],
    "reading_order": 12,
    "text": "In general, a real number  is called a root of the quadratic equation\nax2 + bx + c = 0, a  0 if a 2 + b + c = 0. We also say that x =  is a solution of\nthe quadratic equation, or that  satisfies the quadratic equation. Note that the\nzeroes of the quadratic polynomial ax2 + bx + c and the roots of the quadratic\nequation ax2 + bx + c = 0 are the same.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b13",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      341.01,
      420.07,
      366.47
    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "You have observed, in Chapter 2, that a quadratic polynomial can have at most\ntwo zeroes. So, any quadratic equation can have atmost two roots.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      371.49,
      420.06,
      410.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "You have learnt in Class IX, how to factorise quadratic polynomials by splitting\ntheir middle terms. We shall use this knowledge for finding the roots of a quadratic\nequation. Let us see how.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b15",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      419.79,
      389.35,
      432.47
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "Example 3 : Find the roots of the equation 2x2 – 5x + 3 = 0, by factorisation.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b16",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      47.98,
      437.08,
      419.98,
      462.95
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "Solution : Let us first split the middle term – 5x as –2x –3x [because (–2x) × (–3x) =\n6x2 = (2x2) × 3].",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b17",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      467.73,
      392.16,
      479.99
    ],
    "reading_order": 17,
    "text": "So,\n2x2 – 5x + 3 = 2x2 – 2x – 3x + 3 = 2x (x – 1) –3(x – 1) = (2x – 3)(x – 1)",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b18",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      485.01,
      322.55,
      497.27
    ],
    "reading_order": 18,
    "text": "Now, 2x2 – 5x + 3 = 0 can be rewritten as (2x – 3)(x – 1) = 0.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b19",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      47.98,
      502.29,
      419.99,
      527.75
    ],
    "reading_order": 19,
    "text": "So, the values of x for which 2x2 – 5x + 3 = 0 are the same for which (2x – 3)(x – 1) = 0,\ni.e., either 2x – 3 = 0 or x – 1 = 0.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b20",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      47.99,
      528.84,
      292.56,
      557.05
    ],
    "reading_order": 20,
    "text": "Now, 2x – 3 = 0 gives \n3\n2\nx \n and x – 1 = 0 gives x = 1.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh104_p005_b21",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      552.12,
      283.46,
      580.33
    ],
    "reading_order": 21,
    "text": "So, \n3\n2\nx \n and x = 1 are the solutions of the equation.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH104",
